document offers\n   a solution to these shortcomings for compatible peers by using a\n   section of the TLS \"EC Named Curve Registry\" to establish common\n   finite-field DH parameters with known structure and a mechanism for\n   peers to negotiate support for these groups.\n", "ad": null, "doc": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-tls-negotiated-ff-dhe/", "expires": "2015-05-17T03:56:17Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1326/", "id": 68452, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ietf-tls-negotiated-ff-dhe", "note": "", "notify": "\"Sean Turner\" <turners@ieca.com>", "pages": 22, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/dochistory/68452/", "rev": "03", "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": "/api/v1/person/email/turners@ieca.com/", "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/", "/api/v1/doc/state/38/"], "std_level": null, "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "tags": [], "time": "2014-11-13T04:56:17Z", "title": "Negotiated Finite Field Diffie-Hellman Ephemeral Parameters for TLS", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   Traditional finite-field-based Diffie-Hellman (DH) key exchange\n   during the TLS handshake suffers from a number of security,\n   interoperability, and efficiency shortcomings.  These shortcomings\n   arise from lack of clarity about which DH group parameters TLS\n   servers should offer and clients should accept.  This document offers\n   a solution to these shortcomings for compatible peers by using a\n   section of the TLS \"EC Named Curve Registry\" to establish common\n   finite-field DH parameters with known structure and a mechanism for\n   peers to negotiate support for these groups.\n", "ad": null, "doc": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-tls-negotiated-ff-dhe/", "expires": "2015-06-08T17:41:07Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1326/", "id": 69285, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ietf-tls-negot